Tinea Pedis
Common Infections and Afflictions
Interdigital scaling and maceration, Intense pruritus, Hyperkeratotic soles

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: CIA-043
Reason for Visit: Excruciating foot itch and weeping between the toes.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient reporting acute, intense pruritus and painful "weeping" fissures between the toes, significantly worsening over the past 24 hours. The patient is unable to tolerate wearing shoes due to severe burning and is showing signs of localized inflammation. Urgent home assessment is mandated to perform a thorough podiatric evaluation, definitively rule out evolving secondary bacterial cellulitis or an infected diabetic foot ulcer, and initiate immediate antifungal and antiseptic therapy. Delaying this assessment poses an unacceptable risk of allowing a superficial fungal infection to progress into a deep tissue bacterial infection, fulfilling the criteria for an urgent after-hours MBS assessment (MBS 585-600).

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My feet are burning and itching so badly I can't even put socks on, and the skin between my toes is raw and leaking.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms initiated 5 days ago as mild redness but have sharply escalated. The patient reports severe erytheum and scaling strictly in the interdigital web spaces. Significant maceration and painful fissuring between the toes are present. The patient experiences intense pruritus that is heavily exacerbated by removing footwear. Notes a persistent, unpleasant foot odor and a sharp burning sensation upon exposing the feet to air. History of frequenting communal showers at a local gym. Denies systemic fevers, rigors, or previous history of diabetes mellitus.
Functional Status: Independent, but currently restricted to barefoot ambulation within the home.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone. High situational distress.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 128/84 | HR: 80 | Temp: 37.1 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Alert, well-perfused. Appears uncomfortable, sitting with feet exposed.
Recent/Relevant Labs: None available. A positive KOH preparation showing branching dermatophyte hyphae would assist in confirming the diagnosis, but is not acutely indicated over clinical findings.
Physical Exam: Dermatological examination of both feet reveals extensive interdigital scaling and white, "soggy" maceration, particularly between the 4th and 5th digits. Deep, painful fissures are visible in the web spaces with minor serous oozing. The soles and lateral borders of the feet exhibit thickened, hyperkeratotic (moccasin-type) skin. There is no spreading warmth, intense fluctuance, or ascending red streaks (ruling out acute cellulitis). Pedal pulses are strong and equal. Sensation is intact.
Environmental Observations: Damp gym bag and sweaty sneakers visible near the door.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications. Patient has applied a generic talcum powder which has clumped in the fissures, exacerbating the irritation.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Tinea Pedis - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:
Secondary Bacterial Cellulitis - Ruled out by the absence of spreading, non-demarcated erythema, lack of systemic fever, and the localized nature of the inflammation within the web spaces.
Contact Dermatitis (Shoe/Sock Allergy) - Unlikely given the specific interdigital maceration and history of gym shower exposure, which is pathognomonic for fungal infection.
Erythrasma - Ruled out as the lesions do not exhibit the characteristic coral-red fluorescence under Wood's lamp (if performed) and are scaly rather than smooth and reddish-brown.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY
The acute presentation of painful fissures and intense pruritus necessitated an urgent evaluation to exclude an evolving bacterial superinfection. Direct physical examination confirmed a severe exacerbation of Tinea Pedis (Athlete's Foot) with significant maceration but no current signs of cellulitis. Immediate intervention with topical antifungals and strict moisture-control protocols was required to stop the fungal replication and prevent the fissures from becoming entry points for a systemic bacterial infection. This time-critical stabilization could not safely wait until the next standard operating period, satisfying MBS 585-600 criteria.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed Terbinafine 1% cream to be applied to all affected areas and the entire sole twice daily for 2 weeks. Advised twice-daily foot soaks in a dilute antiseptic solution (e.g., Povidone-iodine) for 5 minutes to dry the macerated skin and treat the fissures.
Positive Behavioral Change: To physically prevent the fungus from surviving, you must rotate your footwear and never wear the same pair of shoes two days in a row. It takes exactly 24 to 48 hours for a shoe to completely dry out internally; wearing damp shoes provides a permanent, warm incubator that protects the fungus from your medication.
Home-Based Services: None.
DME/Safety: None.
Follow-up: Review with regular General Practitioner in 1 week to ensure fissures are closing.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN
Present to the Emergency Department immediately if the redness begins to spread rapidly up the top of your foot or leg, if the area becomes intensely hot and painful, or if you develop a fever.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical; secondary bacterial infection is a common complication if fissures are not treated.
Clinical Course: The intense itch will subside within 3-4 days. The fissures will take 7-10 days to heal completely. Antifungal treatment must continue for at least one week after the skin appears clear to kill dormant spores.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE
Seek medical review if the skin between your toes begins to leak thick, yellow pus or if a foul-smelling discharge develops.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: This is a fungal infection that thrives in dark, damp environments. The cream kills the fungus, but the antiseptic soaks are vital to "dry out" the soggy skin and allow the fissures to close.
Actionable Steps: Use a separate towel strictly for your feet and dry between every toe meticulously after every shower. Wear 100% cotton socks and change them immediately if they become damp.

